    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1de5aaf8-5a1f-5e4b-8a31-7887395ff4d7",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={6}^{-7x+2}$ at the point where $x=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={6}^{u},\\,u=-7x+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={6}^{u}\\ln(6),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={6}^{u}\\ln(6)\\cdot (-7) \\\\ & =(-7)\\cdot {6}^{u}\\ln(6) \\\\ & =-7\\cdot {6}^{-7x+2}\\ln(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=0} & =(-7)\\cdot {6}^{-7(0)+2}\\ln(6) \\\\ & =-7\\cdot {6}^{2}\\ln(6) \\\\ & =- 252 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is $- 252 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=0$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={6}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(36)&=(- 252 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)})\\left(x-(0)\\right) \\\\ y&=- 252 x \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)} + 36.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
